-- title:
--   Farkas' lemma: exactly one of Ax = b, x ≥ 0 and Aᵀp ≥ 0, pᵀb < 0 is solvable
-- statement:
--   **Farkas' lemma.** Let $A \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times n}$ and $b \in \mathbb{R}^m$. Exactly one of the following two systems is solvable:
--
--   $$\text{(a)}\quad Ax = b,\ x \ge 0 \qquad\text{or}\qquad \text{(b)}\quad A^{\mathsf T} p \ge 0,\ p^{\mathsf T} b < 0 .$$
--
--   Equivalently, $b$ lies in the cone generated by the columns of $A$ precisely when every vector $p$ having nonnegative inner product with each column also has nonnegative inner product with $b$.
--
--   This transposition theorem is the algebraic core of linear programming: duality, the optimality conditions for a linear program in standard form, and the description of the normal cone of $\{x : Ax=b,\ x \ge 0\}$ all follow from it. It is Theorem 4.6 (p. 165) of Bertsimas & Tsitsiklis, *Introduction to Linear Optimization*.
--
--   **Formalization note.** `Xor` is exclusive disjunction, so the statement asserts both that the alternatives are incompatible and that one of them holds. Vectors are functions out of `Fin n`, and `0 ≤ x` is the pointwise order. The statement is the finitely generated (column) form of Farkas' lemma; it is not a specialization of the Hilbert-space separation theorem for closed convex cones, because closedness of the column cone is part of what has to be established.
-- source:
--   D. Bertsimas and J. N. Tsitsiklis, Introduction to Linear Optimization, Athena Scientific 1997, Section 4.6, Theorem 4.6, p. 165

import Mathlib

open Matrix

theorem Polyhedral.farkas_lemma {m n : ℕ} (A : Matrix (Fin m) (Fin n) ℝ)
    (b : Fin m → ℝ) :
    Xor (∃ x : Fin n → ℝ, 0 ≤ x ∧ A.mulVec x = b)
      (∃ p : Fin m → ℝ, 0 ≤ Aᵀ.mulVec p ∧ p ⬝ᵥ b < 0) := by sorry
